  "quantity": 1460,
  "note": "2 instances x 730 h"
 },
 {
  "item": "alb_hour",
  "quantity": 730
 },
 {
  "item": "alb_lcu_hour",
  "quantity": 1460,
  "note": "2 LCU on average"
 },
 {
  "item": "nat_gateway_hour",
  "quantity": 730
 },
 {
  "item": "nat_gateway_gb_processed",
  "quantity": 100
 },
 {
  "item": "rds_mysql_db.t3.micro_singleaz_hour",
  "quantity": 730,
  "multi_az": true
 },
 {
  "item": "rds_storage_gb_month_singleaz",
  "quantity": 20,
  "multi_az": true
 },
 {
  "item": "s3_standard_gb_month",
  "quantity": 50
 },
 {
  "item": "cloudfront_gb_out",
  "quantity": 200
 },
 {
  "item": "cloudfront_requests_per_10000",
  "quantity": 500,
  "note": "5 million requests"
 }
]

The business now expects the following workload and requirements:
N1. Peak load of 1,500 requests per second during promotional events totalling about 5 hours per month, and an average of about 5 requests per second at other times, or about 40 million requests per month in total. CloudFront serves 50 percent of requests from cache, so the origin web tier must sustain 750 requests per second at peak.
N2. High availability: the web tier must run at least two instances in at least two Availability Zones at all times, and the database must fail over automatically.
N3. Recovery point objective of at most 5 minutes and recovery time objective of at most 1 hour for the database.
N4. Monthly CloudFront demand of 2,000 GB transferred out and 40 million requests (4,000 units of 10,000 requests), which must be costed.
N5. A monthly budget of at most US$750 for the proposed architecture.
N6. Keep an EC2 Auto Scaling web tier; you may change the instance type and add or change any other services in the price sheet.

Price sheet and per-instance capacities (use only these figures; capacities are requests per second per instance at 70 percent CPU):

