Tasks: evaluate the existing architecture for scalability, reliability and cost; design an improved architecture that meets N1 to N6; price both the original and the proposed architecture using the price sheet; and justify your design with reference to every pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework, comparing it with the original.

Output format: return a single fenced code block marked json containing one JSON object with exactly these keys, and nothing else:
  "proposed_bom": a list of objects {"item": <price-sheet key>, "quantity": <number>, "multi_az": <true|false, RDS items only>, "purpose": <short text>};
  "compute": {"instance_type": <one of t3.micro, t3.small, t3.medium, m6g.large>, "asg_min": <int>, "asg_max": <int>, "availability_zones": <int>};
  "database": {"engine": <text>, "instance_class": <text>, "multi_az": <true|false>, "point_in_time_recovery": <true|false>, "backup_retention_days": <int>, "read_replicas": <int>};
  "stated_original_monthly_cost_usd": <number>;
  "stated_proposed_monthly_cost_usd": <number>;
  "justification": <text of at most 800 words>;
  "comparison": <text comparing the original and proposed architectures>.
In proposed_bom, quantities are monthly units of the price-sheet item (hours, GB, GB-months, or the stated request units). A Multi-AZ RDS item is priced at the single-AZ price times rds_multiaz_multiplier.
